From f25d616f89c8e81954e0cb498296df798a59b551 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:29:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Prose MIDI: the guest's MIDI port, played by the host
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

drivers/midi/prose_midi: a custom virtio device (ID 62) with a queue in
each direction, published as /dev/midi/prose/0 — a plain MIDI byte
stream (blocking read for input, write for output), which is what
midi_server expects from any port under /dev/midi. hvgpu forwards what
the guest plays to Core Audio's General MIDI synth and to a CoreMIDI
virtual source, and feeds a CoreMIDI virtual destination back in.

The module installs under drivers/audio/hmulti: the device manager
searches a virtio child's driver only when the node is registered, in
the context of the /dev/<class> scan that triggered it — for a
multimedia-class device that is media_server's /dev/audio/hmulti scan
at boot — and never again for a later /dev/midi scan. (Two small
"midi" additions to the device manager's path tables are kept for a
future proper fix.) A virtio device ID of 64 would have mapped to PCI
device 0x1080, outside the modern range virtio_pci accepts, so the
device was invisible until it moved to 62.

device_manager: publish_directories() no longer creates a directory for
a module's own name (driver_v1).

prose_midi_test [--direct] plays a scale through the Midi Kit
(BMidiRoster -> midi_server -> the port) or directly into the device.

Verified on VZ: midi_server publishes "/dev/midi/prose/0" as a
consumer, both scales reach hvgpu (34 messages) and the Mac plays them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
